module

public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.IdealSheaf.Functorial
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.DivisorFlatPullback.Basic

/-!
# The inverse image of an ideal sheaf is multiplicative, and square-zero closed immersions

The scheme-theoretic inverse image `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.comap` of an ideal
sheaf is the ideal sheaf generated by the pullback of the sections, so it commutes with products
and powers of ideal sheaves. Mathlib records only its lattice-theoretic properties — it preserves
`⊥`, `⊤` and suprema, being the lower adjoint of a Galois connection — and the semiring structure
on ideal sheaves is not reached by those. This file supplies the multiplicativity, and draws the
consequence that a base change of a closed immersion with nilpotent (in particular square-zero)
kernel ideal sheaf again has nilpotent (square-zero) kernel ideal sheaf.

## Main results

* `Stacks.IdealSheaf.comap_mul`, `Stacks.IdealSheaf.comap_finset_prod` and
  `Stacks.IdealSheaf.comap_pow`: the inverse image of an
  ideal sheaf along an arbitrary morphism of schemes commutes with products and with powers.
* `Stacks.IdealSheaf.isNilpotent_comap`: the inverse image of a nilpotent ideal sheaf is
  nilpotent.
* `Stacks.IdealSheaf.ker_pullback_fst_sq_eq_bot`: **a base change of a square-zero closed
  immersion is square-zero.** For a closed immersion `i` with `i.ker ^ 2 = ⊥` and any `f` with
  the same target, `(Limits.pullback.fst f i).ker ^ 2 = ⊥`.
* `Stacks.IdealSheaf.isNilpotent_ker_pullback_fst`: the same statement for a nilpotent kernel
  ideal sheaf, which is the hypothesis shape that
  `AlgebraicGeometry.FormallyUnramified.hom_ext` takes.

## Implementation notes

Multiplicativity is not formal: the Galois connection
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.map_gc` characterises `comap I f` from above, by
`comap I f ≤ J ↔ I ≤ map J f`, and that characterisation together with the inequality
`map A f * map B f ≤ map (A * B) f` for the direct image yields only
`(I * J).comap f ≤ I.comap f * J.comap f`. A transfer of nilpotence needs the reverse
inclusion `(I.comap f) ^ 2 ≤ (I ^ 2).comap f`, which the Galois connection does not give. So
the proof is affine-local, through
`Stacks.IdealSheaf.ideal_comap_appLE`: on an affine open `V` of the source contained in the
preimage of an affine open `U` of the target, the ideal of `I.comap f` is `Ideal.map` of the
ideal of `I` on `U` along `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.appLE`, and `Ideal.map` is a semiring
homomorphism on ideals (`Ideal.map_mul`, `Ideal.map_pow`). Such pairs of charts exist around
every point of the source (`Stacks.IdealSheaf.exists_affineOpens_le_preimage`), so the charts
`V` cover the source and `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.ext_of_iSup_eq_top` applies.
No affineness or quasi-compactness hypothesis on the morphism is needed, in contrast with
`Stacks.IdealSheaf.ideal_comap_of_isAffineHom`.

The geometric statements are then immediate from Mathlib's
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.ker_fst_of_isClosedImmersion`, which identifies the
kernel ideal sheaf of a base change of a closed immersion with the inverse image of its kernel
ideal sheaf. Both projections are covered: `Limits.pullback.fst f i` lands in the source of `f`
and `Limits.pullback.snd i f` lands in the source of `f` as well, for the transposed fibre
product, and the two are compared by `CategoryTheory.Limits.pullbackSymmetry`.

Nothing here carries a Stacks tag. The base-change statement for thickenings is Stacks Project
Tag 09ZU, which is a statement about thickenings rather than about ideal sheaves, and this
module supplies only its ideal-sheaf core; the tag belongs on the thickening-level statements.
Tag 04EX, which defines thickenings and their orders, states no base-change property. The two
repository developments of 04EX (`Stacks.Thickenings.IsFirstOrderThickening` and
`Stacks.Thickenings.IsFirstOrderThickening`) are abbreviations for the conjunction
`IsClosedImmersion i ∧ i.ker ^ 2 = ⊥`; neither is imported here, and a consumer of either
obtains the base-changed thickening as `⟨inferInstance, ker_pullback_fst_sq_eq_bot i f h⟩`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 04EX](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04EX)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 04F1](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04F1)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 09ZU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09ZU)
-/

@[expose] public section

open AlgebraicGeometry CategoryTheory

universe u

namespace Stacks.IdealSheaf

section Comap

variable {X Y : Scheme.{u}}

/-- Around every point of the source of a morphism of schemes there is an affine open of the
source contained in the preimage of an affine open of the target. -/
theorem exists_affineOpens_le_preimage (f : Y ⟶ X) (y : Y) :
    ∃ (U : X.affineOpens) (V : Y.affineOpens),
      (V : Y.Opens) ≤ f ⁻¹ᵁ (U : X.Opens) ∧ y ∈ (V : Y.Opens) := by
  obtain ⟨U, hU, hyU, -⟩ := TopologicalSpace.Opens.isBasis_iff_nbhd.mp X.isBasis_affineOpens
    (show f y ∈ (⊤ : X.Opens) from trivial)
  obtain ⟨V, hV, hyV, hVU⟩ := TopologicalSpace.Opens.isBasis_iff_nbhd.mp Y.isBasis_affineOpens
    (show y ∈ f ⁻¹ᵁ U from hyU)
  exact ⟨⟨U, hU⟩, ⟨V, hV⟩, hVU, hyV⟩

/-- Two ideal sheaves on the source of a morphism are equal when their ideals agree on every
affine open of the source contained in the preimage of an affine open of the target. -/
private lemma ext_of_forall_le_preimage (f : Y ⟶ X) {A B : Y.IdealSheafData}
    (h : ∀ (U : X.affineOpens) (V : Y.affineOpens), (V : Y.Opens) ≤ f ⁻¹ᵁ (U : X.Opens) →
      A.ideal V = B.ideal V) : A = B := by
  refine Scheme.IdealSheafData.ext_of_iSup_eq_top (ι := {p : X.affineOpens × Y.affineOpens //
    (p.2 : Y.Opens) ≤ f ⁻¹ᵁ (p.1 : X.Opens)}) (fun p => p.1.2)
    (eq_top_iff.2 fun y _ => ?_) fun p => h _ _ p.2
  obtain ⟨U, V, hVU, hyV⟩ := exists_affineOpens_le_preimage f y
  exact TopologicalSpace.Opens.mem_iSup.mpr ⟨⟨(U, V), hVU⟩, hyV⟩

/-- **The inverse image of ideal sheaves is multiplicative.** The scheme-theoretic inverse image
of a product of two ideal sheaves along an arbitrary morphism of schemes is the product of their
inverse images. -/
theorem comap_mul (I J : X.IdealSheafData) (f : Y ⟶ X) :
    (I * J).comap f = I.comap f * J.comap f := by
  refine ext_of_forall_le_preimage f fun U V h => ?_
  simp only [ideal_comap_appLE _ _ U V h, Scheme.IdealSheafData.ideal_mul, Pi.mul_apply,
    Ideal.map_mul]

/-- **The inverse image of ideal sheaves commutes with finite products.**  Iterating
`Stacks.IdealSheaf.comap_mul` over a finite index set. -/
theorem comap_finset_prod {ι : Type*} (s : Finset ι) (I : ι → X.IdealSheafData) (f : Y ⟶ X) :
    (∏ i ∈ s, I i).comap f = ∏ i ∈ s, (I i).comap f := by
  classical
  induction s using Finset.induction_on with
  | empty =>
    simpa only [Finset.prod_empty, Scheme.IdealSheafData.one_eq_top] using
      Scheme.IdealSheafData.comap_top f
  | insert a t ha ih =>
    rw [Finset.prod_insert ha, Finset.prod_insert ha, comap_mul, ih]

/-- **The inverse image of ideal sheaves commutes with powers.** The scheme-theoretic inverse
image of a power of an ideal sheaf along an arbitrary morphism of schemes is that power of its
inverse image. -/
theorem comap_pow (I : X.IdealSheafData) (f : Y ⟶ X) (n : ℕ) :
    (I ^ n).comap f = I.comap f ^ n := by
  refine ext_of_forall_le_preimage f fun U V h => ?_
  simp only [ideal_comap_appLE _ _ U V h, Scheme.IdealSheafData.ideal_pow, Pi.pow_apply,
    Ideal.map_pow]

/-- The scheme-theoretic inverse image of a nilpotent ideal sheaf is nilpotent. -/
theorem isNilpotent_comap {I : X.IdealSheafData} (f : Y ⟶ X) (hI : IsNilpotent I) :
    IsNilpotent (I.comap f) := by
  obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ := hI
  exact ⟨n, by rw [← comap_pow, hn, Scheme.IdealSheafData.zero_eq_bot,
    Scheme.IdealSheafData.comap_bot, Scheme.IdealSheafData.zero_eq_bot]⟩

end Comap

section BaseChange

open Limits

variable {X Y Z : Scheme.{u}}

/-- The kernel ideal sheaf of a base change of a closed immersion `i : Z ⟶ Y` along
`f : X ⟶ Y`, raised to a power, is the inverse image along `f` of that power of the kernel ideal
sheaf of `i`. -/
theorem ker_pullback_fst_pow (i : Z ⟶ Y) [IsClosedImmersion i] (f : X ⟶ Y) (n : ℕ) :
    (pullback.fst f i).ker ^ n = (i.ker ^ n).comap f := by
  rw [Scheme.IdealSheafData.ker_fst_of_isClosedImmersion, comap_pow]

/-- If a power of the kernel ideal sheaf of a closed immersion `i : Z ⟶ Y` vanishes, then the
same power of the kernel ideal sheaf of its base change along any `f : X ⟶ Y` vanishes. -/
theorem ker_pullback_fst_pow_eq_bot (i : Z ⟶ Y) [IsClosedImmersion i] (f : X ⟶ Y) {n : ℕ}
    (h : i.ker ^ n = ⊥) : (pullback.fst f i).ker ^ n = ⊥ := by
  rw [ker_pullback_fst_pow, h, Scheme.IdealSheafData.comap_bot]

/-- **A base change of a square-zero closed immersion is square-zero.** If the kernel ideal
sheaf of a closed immersion `i : Z ⟶ Y` has vanishing square, then so does the kernel ideal
sheaf of its base change along any `f : X ⟶ Y`. -/
theorem ker_pullback_fst_sq_eq_bot (i : Z ⟶ Y) [IsClosedImmersion i] (f : X ⟶ Y)
    (h : i.ker ^ 2 = ⊥) : (pullback.fst f i).ker ^ 2 = ⊥ :=
  ker_pullback_fst_pow_eq_bot i f h

/-- A base change of a closed immersion with nilpotent kernel ideal sheaf again has nilpotent
kernel ideal sheaf. This is the hypothesis shape taken by
`AlgebraicGeometry.FormallyUnramified.hom_ext`. -/
theorem isNilpotent_ker_pullback_fst (i : Z ⟶ Y) [IsClosedImmersion i] (f : X ⟶ Y)
    (h : IsNilpotent i.ker) : IsNilpotent (pullback.fst f i).ker := by
  rw [Scheme.IdealSheafData.ker_fst_of_isClosedImmersion]
  exact isNilpotent_comap f h

/-- The kernel ideal sheaf of the second projection of the transposed fibre product of a closed
immersion `i : Z ⟶ Y` along `f : X ⟶ Y` is the inverse image along `f` of the kernel ideal sheaf
of `i`. -/
theorem ker_pullback_snd (i : Z ⟶ Y) [IsClosedImmersion i] (f : X ⟶ Y) :
    (pullback.snd i f).ker = i.ker.comap f := by
  rw [← Scheme.IdealSheafData.ker_fst_of_isClosedImmersion i f,
    ← Scheme.Hom.ker_comp_of_isIso (pullbackSymmetry i f).hom (pullback.fst f i),
    pullbackSymmetry_hom_comp_fst]

/-- If a power of the kernel ideal sheaf of a closed immersion `i : Z ⟶ Y` vanishes, then the
same power of the kernel ideal sheaf of the second projection of the transposed fibre product
`Limits.pullback i f` vanishes. -/
theorem ker_pullback_snd_pow_eq_bot (i : Z ⟶ Y) [IsClosedImmersion i] (f : X ⟶ Y) {n : ℕ}
    (h : i.ker ^ n = ⊥) : (pullback.snd i f).ker ^ n = ⊥ := by
  rw [ker_pullback_snd, ← comap_pow, h, Scheme.IdealSheafData.comap_bot]

/-- A base change of a closed immersion with nilpotent kernel ideal sheaf, taken in the
transposed orientation, again has nilpotent kernel ideal sheaf. -/
theorem isNilpotent_ker_pullback_snd (i : Z ⟶ Y) [IsClosedImmersion i] (f : X ⟶ Y)
    (h : IsNilpotent i.ker) : IsNilpotent (pullback.snd i f).ker := by
  rw [ker_pullback_snd]
  exact isNilpotent_comap f h

end BaseChange

end Stacks.IdealSheaf

end
